22:25:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Ah, I see the confusion! You're absolutely right that "pressurized" sounds like it means the pressure is kept perfectly constant—but in airplane lingo, it means something slightly different. Let me clear that up. ### "Pressurized" doesn't mean "constant sea-level pressure" A **pressurized cabin** simply means the air inside the plane is deliberately kept at a *higher pressure* than the outside air at that altitude. It does *not* mean the pressure is identical to what you'd feel at sea level all the time. Think of it this way: - At 35,000 ft, the outside air pressure is only about 25% of sea-level pressure—so low you'd pass out in seconds. - The plane's systems pump in outside air and compress it, raising the cabin pressure to a *safe* level. But for structural reasons, they don't pump it all the way back to sea-level pressure. - So "pressurized" means "kept at a pressure high enough to keep you alive and comfortable," not "fixed at the pressure you had on the ground." ### What actually happens during a flight: - **On the ground (before takeoff)**: The cabin is at the same pressure as outside—whatever the airfield's elevation and weather dictate. If you're at a sea-level airport, that's sea-level pressure. - **During climb**: The cabin pressure is *gradually reduced* by the computer-controlled outflow valves until it reaches an equivalent of about 6,000–8,000 ft altitude. That's the "cabin altitude" you'll have for most of the cruise. - **Cruise**: The pressure holds steady at that cabin altitude (so it is constant *during this phase*, just not at sea level). - **During descent**: The pressure is gradually increased back toward the landing airport's pressure. That's why your ears pop mainly during climb and descent—the pressure *is* changing, just not as wildly as outside. If the pressure were truly constant at sea level the whole flight, your ears would never pop after the first few minutes. But because the cabin altitude is changing, your ears notice the difference. ### Why not keep it at sea level then? It's possible, but it would require a stronger, heavier fuselage to withstand the larger pressure difference. For most airliners, it's a trade-off: a cabin altitude of 6,000–8,000 ft is perfectly safe for almost everyone, keeps the popping manageable, and saves fuel and maintenance costs. Some newer planes like the Boeing 787 go a bit lower (about 6,000 ft cabin altitude) for more comfort, but still not sea level. So when people say "the plane is pressurized," they mean "you won't die from low oxygen and your ears won't explode." They don't mean the pressure is a perfect clone of what it was on the ground. The next time someone says "it's pressurized so the pressure stays the same," you can now explain that "pressurized" is about safety, not absolute constancy!
